<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Tide Pool Guide: Creatures</title>
  <meta name="description" content="Six common tide pool animals, the zone each one lives in, and how it survives.">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a class="logo" href="index.html">Tide Pool Guide</a>
      <nav aria-label="Main">
        <a href="index.html">Home</a>
        <a href="creatures.html" aria-current="page">Creatures</a>
        <a href="visit.html">Plan a visit</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section class="page-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h1>Six animals you can find</h1>
        <p>Each tag shows the shore zone where you’re most likely to spot the animal.</p>
      </div>
      <svg class="wave" viewBox="0 0 1200 70" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M0,35 C150,70 300,0 450,35 C600,70 750,0 900,35 C1050,70 1125,20 1200,35 L1200,70 L0,70 Z"/>
      </svg>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <article class="creature">
          <h3>Acorn barnacle</h3>
          <p class="latin">Balanus glandula</p>
          <span class="tag high">High zone</span>
          <div class="info">
            <p>Barnacles glue themselves to rock for life. When the tide leaves, they close their shell plates tightly to trap water inside.</p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="creature">
          <h3>California mussel</h3>
          <p class="latin">Mytilus californianus</p>
          <span class="tag mid">Middle zone</span>
          <div class="info">
            <p>Mussels grip the rock with strong threads and form thick beds. They filter tiny food from the water and are a favorite meal of sea stars.</p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="creature">
          <h3>Hermit crab</h3>
          <p class="latin">Pagurus samuelis</p>
          <span class="tag mid">Middle zone</span>
          <div class="info">
            <p>A hermit crab has a soft body, so it lives in an empty snail shell and trades up to a bigger one as it grows. Watch the pools for shells that seem to walk.</p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="creature">
          <h3>Ochre sea star</h3>
          <p class="latin">Pisaster ochraceus</p>
          <span class="tag mid">Middle to low zone</span>
          <div class="info">
            <p>This purple or orange sea star pulls mussels apart with its arms. Keeping mussels in check leaves room for many other species, which is why scientists call it a keystone predator.</p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="creature">
          <h3>Giant green anemone</h3>
          <p class="latin">Anthopleura xanthogrammica</p>
          <span class="tag low">Low zone</span>
          <div class="info">
            <p>Its green color comes from algae living inside its tissue. It catches small prey on sticky tentacles and folds them in. Please look without touching.</p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="creature">
          <h3>Purple sea urchin</h3>
          <p class="latin">Strongylocentrotus purpuratus</p>
          <span class="tag low">Low zone</span>
          <div class="info">
            <p>Urchins scrape algae off rock with five tiny teeth. Over many years they can wear small bowls into the stone that they then shelter in.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <div class="wrap"><p>Tide Pool Guide is a college web design project.</p></div>
  </footer>
</body>
</html>
